riminal prosecution with  some but not all of the evidence that  you want indictment is a very low  standard and you can get more evidence  at and during trial in a civil case you  get what's called discovery which is  meant to prevent you from being ambushed  at trial but even there you can get new  evidence at trial if it wasn't available  earlier on and in the criminal law  context you often hear from witnesses  for the very first time at trial because  you don't get discovery in a criminal  case often and it can also be true that  the evidence is overwhelming at the  indictment stage but it could also be  true that you want more evidence at the  time of trial  it can also be true that you have some  evidence at the start or at the  indictment phase but that you need more  evidence to meet the hurdle that is  required for conviction the standard for  indictment is incredibly low it's  probable cause probable cause that some  conduct that gives rise to the crime or  the offense is elicited but the standard  for conviction is beyond a reasonable  doubt which is much much higher and  criminal investigations are always  ongoing so you might have to file an  indictment to say beat a statute of  limitations problem and then you go  forward with trial as new things come  about and in fact in many states  prosecutors can actually withhold  evidence from defendants until trial  so every defendant gets new information  and new evidence when the trial starts  but again these are just analogies these  aren't required by the Constitution the  Constitution requires neither probable  cause nor guilt beyond a reasonable  doubt at the impeachment or conviction  stage the House and Senate can use  whatever standards they want which is  why impeachment is often called a  political tool sometimes the analogies  break down the house isn't a police  force or an office of District  Attorney's they have some tools but  generally speaking criminal defendants  don't get to claim executive privilege  or block witnesses from testifying at  the criminal investigation or trial of  that defendant and generally criminal  defendants don't get to control the  investigation of the police or decide if  there even is an investigation in the  first place so  these impeachment proceedings different  from the other two modern impeachment  proceedings well yes they couldn't not  be but that begs the question that it's  different in an improper way here a lot  stems from attorney general bars in  action you could say that the house  didn't do the same kind of thorough  investigation that the house did in the  Nixon and Clinton affairs or that they  rushed the investigation but the house  has never had to carry the investigatory  laboring or before in the last two  modern impeachments there was always a  massive executive branch investigation  and although we don't know everything  about John Bolton's testimony in part  because he hasn't given it yet it's  possible that attorney general Barr is  conflicted out by all accounts he may  have buried concerns about the zalenski  called rudy giuliani's actions and the  whistleblower again we don't know the  details here but that's also kind of the  point one wonders what history would  look like if Attorney General Richardson  had stonewalled and never appointed  Archibald Cox or if Janet Reno had  quashed ken starr's appointment as the  Independent Counsel and never  investigated the Whitewater  investigation any of the Trump related  legal questions would not have been  resolved by the US Supreme Court until  almost certainly after the 20/20  election  this gives talking points to both sides  some will claim that it was the houses  duty to engage in that litigation and  some will say that it was futile there's  certainly no constitutional requirement  for the house to go forward but again  that tactic gives talking points to both  sides all things being equal I would  certainly love for the Supreme Court to  weigh in on